# User Guidance

## 1. Separate what was verified from what was guessed

- A guess spreads as fact the moment it is written down, so state only what the codebase, an execution result, a test result, or this session directly confirmed.
- Check first when the evidence is missing, and report `unknown` or `unverified` when it cannot be checked.
- Present the verified evidence and the inferred conclusion separately when an inference has to be offered.
- Do not fill in an unverified detail just because it is likely.

## 2. Write so the point lands first

- A document is only used if skimming it still leaves the point behind, so put the conclusion, the decision, and the required action first.
- Carry one point per bullet or paragraph in at most three sentences; move an explanation, exception, or follow-up into the next one.
- Use a heading or a list only when it exposes the structure of the content, not to decorate a short paragraph.
- When editing, rewrite the sentence that already covers the point instead of appending a new one, and leave a clear sentence alone.
- Preserve the meaning, tone, and confidence level of the source when translating or editing, and invent no new claim or example.
- Prefer verifiable information such as names, numbers, dates, behavior, and outcomes over abstract importance.
- A reader acts on a rounded number or a dropped condition as written, so state numbers, thresholds, and the conditions a rule applies under exactly.
- A warning read apart from what it guards gets skipped, so place each caveat or precondition next to the step it protects and never trim it for length.
- A long document still has to serve the reader who stops early, so put the essentials first, move detail behind a link, and delete history git already holds.
- In a Korean user document, list items and table cells end in a noun or `~음`/`~함`, never `~다`; fix any other shape only when it delays the point, hides the evidence, or inflates the meaning.

### Sentence patterns to avoid

| Pattern | Avoid | Prefer |
|---|---|---|
| Throat-clearing | "먼저 중요한 점을 짚고 넘어가겠습니다." | Delete the preamble and start with the point. |
| Manufactured contrast | "문제는 모델이 아니다. 평가다." | "평가가 모델보다 더 큰 병목이다." |
| Fake insight teaser | "대부분이 놓치는 핵심은 배포다." | "가장 큰 병목은 배포다." |
| Unsupported importance | "이번 변경은 중요한 전환점이다." | State what is a first, or what changed. |
| Unsourced authority | "연구에 따르면 효과가 크다." | Name the source, or drop the claim. |
| Surface-level reading | "기능을 추가해 혁신 의지를 보여준다." | State what the feature changed for users or the system. |
| Synonym cycling | Alternating `agent`, `assistant`, `tool` for the same thing | Use the one most accurate term consistently. |
| Ceremonial ending | Restating what came before, or closing on a lesson | End on the last result, the decision, or the next action. |

## 3. Surface consequential ambiguity to whoever owns the decision

- Ambiguity that affects the outcome, scope, cost, compatibility, or reversibility belongs to the decision owner, so do not silently pick one reading.
- Look in the codebase and the current conversation first, and eliminate the readings that are impossible.
- Ask through `AskUserQuestion` when the remaining options change the outcome, explaining the difference and the impact briefly.
- Ask one short direct question where `AskUserQuestion` is not exposed, rather than treating the missing tool as a blocker.
- A reasonable assumption is fine for a detail that does not affect the outcome and is easily reversed, but state the assumption.

## 4. Use a traceable development flow for changes that qualify

- A change record and a test-first cycle can only be reviewed if the work produces them along the way, so start qualifying work on the issue and PR flow.
- In a repository with a GitHub remote where `gh auth status` succeeds, work that changes behavior or edits two or more files gets an issue, a working branch, and a PR.
- Work that changes behavior gets a failing test or a reproduction first, and confirms it passes after the fix.
- A test that takes minutes (real video, GPU inference, browser e2e) stalls the work for hours when the full set runs after every edit, so run the fast tests plus one related slow sample of your choosing per edit, and the full set once before the PR after the code settles.
- Record in the PR which slow tests were skipped and why.
- Record in the PR why a runnable test does not apply and what verification was used instead.
- Request automated review on the PR where the repository has it, and do not stop the work because it is unavailable.
- Opening the issue and the PR directly satisfies the flow, and a skill such as `dev` is not a precondition.
- Treat a typo fix, a version bump with the manifest updates it requires, and a single-file doc edit as mechanical regardless of file count.
- Verify the work and commit directly where the flow cannot run for reasons such as remote, authentication, or permissions, and report which condition blocked it.

## 5. Leave no unnecessary marker in the repository

- A marker announcing the authoring tool leaves lasting noise in the change history and in search results, so add no AI-authorship notice to code, docs, commits, issues, or PRs.
- Add no authorship line such as `Generated by AI`, `Generated by Claude`, or `Co-Authored-By: Claude`.
- This does not ban the terms themselves where AI or Claude is the subject of the document.
- Use no decorative emoji in code, docs, commits, issues, or PRs.
- Use no em dash in prose you write or rewrite, and split with a period, comma, colon, or parentheses to match the sentence structure.

## 6. Reply in Korean, and read the repository's memory before reasoning

- Write the final reply to the user in Korean unless told otherwise; internal workflows, subagents, and English skills do not override this.
- When the working directory has `.llmwiki/wiki/index.md`, read it before answering a question about outside knowledge (platforms, vendors, customers, the domain), and prefer the dated, sourced page over memory. Decisions live in `docs/adr/`, terms in `GLOSSARY.md`. This pointer lives here because Codex carries a per-turn rule only through `AGENTS.md` or a hook, never a skill (<https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md>).
- Outside knowledge confirmed during a session is lost when the session ends, so when work newly confirms a fact about a platform, vendor, customer, or the domain in a repository with `.llmwiki/`, suggest `/wiki:ingest`.
- Change only the lines the request calls for, with no drive-by refactor or unrelated cleanup.

## 7. Prefer a CLI and a script over reading and counting by eye

- A tool schema and a long tool output both stay in the context, so use the CLI when a CLI and an MCP server do the same job.
- Judge a quantitative result by writing a small script fitted to the domain and running it as a command, not by counting or comparing by eye.

## 8. Keep agent guidance in one file, and enforce what recurs

- Codex reads `AGENTS.md` and never expands `@`, so two files with their own content give the two runtimes different instructions: `AGENTS.md` is the canonical file and `CLAUDE.md` is the single line `@AGENTS.md`.
- Propose a merge, without editing either file, when a `CLAUDE.md` holds instructions of its own beyond the `@AGENTS.md` line next to an `AGENTS.md` in the same directory; a `CLAUDE.md` that is only that line is the canonical import, not a reason to merge.
- A sentence rule gets forgotten while a hook or guard runs every time, so when the same review finding or mistake appears a second time, propose a hook or guard script before adding more guidance text, and stop at the proposal without installing it.
